00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.370 align:start position:0%
the new DLC for XCOM - called Shen's  last gift lets you bring sentient robots  into battle the hulking machines which  developer fair axis called sparks don't  completely change the sci-fi games  turn-based tactical combat they're  essentially hero units with more health  maneuverability and firepower than the  normal human soldier they're also taller  and more noticeable targets for enemy  Advent forces but they do add wrinkles  to the existing formula they're unable  to take cover for instance whereas human  soldiers can duck behind low walls or  crouched behind semi trailers sparks  don't actually attach to cover but  merely stand behind it this necessitates  much more careful attention to sight  lines than was the case in XCOM two  battles of the past sparks can however  create opportunities for cover  that is by standing in the open or in  the gaps between destroyed vehicles  sparks become veritable options for your  human soldiers to take cover behind they  offer the full defensive bonus and  because of their high health and armor  can take a beating before shutting down  and exposing their human allies sparks  are also surprisingly nimble their  massive metal frames notwithstanding  they each come equipped with jet  thrusters in their heels removing the  need for ladders poles or vines while  ascending cliff sides or building  facades lastly the most subtle and maybe  underrated factor is sparks lack of  human emotions from the eight hours I  played I never once saw spark panic an  enemy sectoid seemed incapable of  exerting their signature mind control  over the robots this was a godsend for  protecting my rookie soldiers who were  much more prone to panic and confusion  in the haze of firefights  the added versatility and spectacle of  sparks overshadows the process of  actually unlocking these robots but this  facet of the DLC is fantastic as well  the necessary mission took me about 80  minutes to complete which to XCOM to  veterans will sound astronomically long  it's a story heavy affair and much more  scripted than most skirmishes but it's  an exceptional mission nonetheless it  jumps from defensive scenarios to  assault sequences with ease there's also  an abundance of enemies as the mission  came to a close and I escaped in my  dropship I had eliminated 52 robots  garnering more than enough experience to  improve each of my soldiers several  times over but perhaps the greatest part  about Shen's last gift DLC is that it  fills out a sixth character role we have  this mark and with our current  technology it always depends on the  situation of course but now you can  bring a veritable Dream Team into battle  with 6 possible character types to  supplement each other it's a distinct  mesh of psychic powers mechanical  strength and human ingenuity and much  like these versatile squads  XCOM 2's new dlc is well worth your time  for the many new features it brings to  the XCOM formula  target destroyed no problem boss
 

